00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
so you're only as weak as your  weakest link and that's why in this  video i'm going to be talking about  addressing  weak points and i don't mean  specifically weak muscles although  that's something i'd like to talk about  in future  rather i mean weak points within a given  muscle  because your muscle can be stronger at  some points in the range of motion  than in others and it's by plugging  these gaps that we can make ourselves  far more dynamic and powerful and gain  more control  over our strength and in particular i  want to talk about something called end  range strength which is a cool concept  that describes how much force you're  able to exert  when the muscle is in an elongated  position  so you might think that you know what  your one rep max is for a curl for a  bench press for a squat etc  but in truth you're probably only  actually using 100 effort in any given  muscle  at certain points during that range of  motion during the  trajectory that you carry the weight  through during that exercise  so a good example of this might be the  bicep curl that's a nice simple exercise  so right down at the bottom of the  movement your bicep actually gets  something of a break that's because the  weight is hanging and your joint  actually  takes a fair amount of that weight then  as you curl the weight upwards  you're changing the angle of resistance  so that the actual  involvement of different muscles is  changing once you get up to the top once  again you're able to support that weight  with your elbow joint and thereby you're  not using your bicep half as much as he  would be  at any other point during that motion  moreover if you're using a heavy weight  in particular  chances are you're kind of exploding at  the bottom of the movement  creating a sudden contraction which is  then propelling the weight upwards  meaning that  momentum is actually carrying the weight  through a certain portion of that  movement  this is what we call the strength curve  and thanks to the strength curve we can  develop these kinds of weak points if  you imagine that you're doing this  over and over again and you're kind of  skipping past certain points in that  movement  then it's easy to imagine that there  will be positions at which you  eventually  are not as strong as others this is why  if you're going to fail in a movement  you can get stuck in a certain position  maybe right at the bottom of the squat  or maybe  halfway through the bench press because  you're not used to exerting as much  strength  in that position this is where your  weakest and this is why training those  things and  fixing those weak points can make you  stronger and more controlled throughout  that entire movement  so that's the first thing we're going to  address how can we fix these kinds of  weaknesses well the first  most obvious thing you can do is to slow  the movement right down and use a  lighter weight  instead of trying to skip past the parts  that are difficult instead of ego  lifting  drop to a lower weight and really slow  down that movement not just through the  eccentric  but also through the concentric  [Music]  this is why i love quasi-isometric  exercises which are extremely slow  push-ups and pull-ups  you can likewise do the same thing with  really slow bicep curls using light  weights or  any other movement really using a light  weight  likewise you might want to train a  little bit more using things like cables  cables are different because the angle  of resistance  although different from what you'd use  with a dumbbell or a bench press can  often remain  the same so for example there are ways  to perform a bicep curl using cables  where the resistance is going to be the  same all the way through the movement or  even greater at the point where it's  usually  weaker so lower that weight and then  slowly move through those range of  motions  with the exact same amount of resistance  forcing your muscles to work hard  all the way through the exercise  similarly you can use bands bands don't  have the same resistance all the way  through but they have a changing  resistance  just want to point out at this juncture  that i'm not saying that using explosive  movements of very heavy weights  isn't a useful way to develop strength  i'm saying that you want both you want  that explosiveness  and that kind of slow control the other  point of a movement where we're often  weakest though  as i mentioned at the start of this  video is your end range the end range is  the point where the muscle is the most  elongated  it's at this point and also when it's at  the shortest that the muscle is the  weakest  this is actually one of the reasons that  we maintain a small amount of tension in  our muscles throughout the day called  tonus because if our muscles completely  relaxed and went completely  elongated then it would be much harder  for us to move explosively  when we suddenly need to but sometimes  you do need to be strong in those  elongated positions sometimes you find  yourself in a situation  hanging from a cliff this is something  again that happens to me all the time  hanging from a cliff or perhaps a  blocking a punch at a very unusual angle  where the ability to exert strength at  that strange position that elongated  awkward position comes in handy this is  why a lot of  calisthenics moves look so awesome like  the lalanne push-up  the leland push-up looks gravity-defying  because you need to  have a rigid torso throughout the  movement but also because you're  starting off in a position  that's often the end point for a lot of  other exercises with your arms stretched  out over your head so it's just this  uncomfortable strange position  and that's the first close to how we can  train our end range strength we do it  using exercises that put us in those  compromised positions those elongated  stretched out positions so the lamp  push-up is a great one but another good  example is anything that uses weight to  actually help us get into that position  which is a form of weighted stretching  tons of exercises do this for example a  weighted cossack squat forces you into  that deep side lunge which is excellent  for strengthening yourself  in that lower position then you've got  to get out of that position again same  thing goes for bulgarian split squats  and all kinds of other exercises a  number of  calisthenics movements also incorporate  a really good end range of motion that  goes beyond what you normally use  such as the v-sit which of course trains  that compressive strength  and a correction on my last video the  v-set builds up to  mana not maltese sorry that was just a  slip of the tongue and this will build  up more range of motion  likewise something like planche training  that has your  biceps in that locked out position and  challenges you to use instead your  scapula to push yourself a little bit  further this is the point where  the biceps and the apex pass the baton  over to the back and this is another way  to train that ranges of motion that  you're maybe not used to training  we can also take a lot of cues from  calisthenics and different forms of  mobility training  because endurance strength has a lot to  do with your mobility  so we tend to think of mobility as this  kind of plasticity this  stretchiness of our muscles and our  connective tissues but of course that's  not really  what's going on you don't get stretchier  as you practice stretching  what actually happens is it's more to do  with your nervous system your ability to  strengthen and contract the agonist  muscles the working muscles and to relax  the opposing antagonistic muscles  they're trying to push you  pull you back into that comfortable  position so as you try and reach that  uncomfortable position the muscles that  are working get weaker as they reach the  end range  of their strength meanwhile they have to  pull against the  antagonist muscles that are tightening  up and tensing thinking hey this isn't  right  and eventually that strength just isn't  there and you can't move any further  mobility is different from flexibility  flexibility is your ability to  pull yourself into a position or to use  an external object to get yourself in  that position  whereas mobility is your ability to get  there on your own so mobility is far  more useful  in martial arts in rock climbing parkour  and of course in any form of training  luckily there are lots of ways we can  increase the strength at the end range  whilst at the same time gaining that  control and that mobility  one example of this is something called  a passive end range  hold basically what this involves is  getting yourself into a stretch position  a passive stretch using some kind of  external tool whether that's a band  whether that's a bar or something and  then what you're going to do is going to  let go  and try and hold that position as best  as you can from your own steam  so you might not have been able to get  into that position on your own but now  you're going to try and hold that  position  and that's going to train yourself to be  able to do it again in future  it's essentially a form of overload  training another similar strategy is  something called a lift off get yourself  into a stretch position with your  shoulders  then you're going to gradually lift  yourself off and hold your arms in that  position can also practice  overcoming isometrics in these positions  so get into the position where you're  weak where you're elongated and then try  and push  against that position to go even further  using some kind of obstacle some kind of  object  to force against yourself so for example  you might lift your leg as high as you  can  put your hand on top of it then try and  push as hard as you can to go even  higher  this also shows you just how little  strength you have at that end range  now imagine if you were able to drive  more power into that position  how much more powerful your kicks might  be similarly try standing with your back  against the wall  lifting your arms overhead but then also  pushing your hands against the wall  to try and get them even further back a  tip when performing this  is to contract your abs and to move the  lower spine towards the floor or against  the wall and by doing this  you're preventing yourself from cheating  essentially by bending your back  instead of using that range of motion in  the shoulders eventually you gain  more strength and control in those end  ranges which not only  helps you to exert more power in unusual  positions  but at the same time helps to move more  freely and with less restriction  this can even help to reduce the risk of  injury because of course you're gaining  more control and more strength  right at the positions where you're the  most vulnerable another point is  when you're right at the top the apex of  the movement really  squeeze the working muscle that way you  are also  exerting a lot of strength during the  shortest most compressed position  that the muscle is in which is also an  area where we're normally very weak  of course make sure to ease yourself  into this kind of training gently don't  force yourself into positions that feel  very uncomfortable or painful  and at the same time make sure that  you're nice and warm before you start  getting into those positions  so there you go guys those are some  ideas and some strategies you can use to  strengthen your muscles at positions  where they're normally  the weakest let me know in the comments  down below if you found this useful and  what strategies you use  to increase your end range strength and  to address weak points in your range of  motion  if you found this video useful and  interesting then please leave a like and  share around that helps me out immensely  subscribe if you want to see more like  this  and if you like this kind of approach to  training where we look at things that  are  maybe 